      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Research in this area is often debated, with many scholars arguing that population differences in behavior are better explained by local adaptations to environmental pressures rather than fixed genetic markers tied to modern ethnicity. Theoretical frameworks like life-history theory suggest that ancestral selection pressures on risk-taking and social cohesion varied across human groups, but these traits are highly plastic and depend heavily on cultural context. Despite some studies attempting to identify genetic signatures linked to specific behavioral tendencies, the field largely emphasizes the complex interplay of gene-culture coevolution over simple ethnic determinism. Consequently, most contemporary literature advocates for caution when generalizing behavioral phenotypes to specific ancestral lineages due to the significant role of epigenetics and environmental factors.",
